 to hear to see what he has  to say tonight  all right we're joined now by mark  morial the national urban league  president and ceo  and former mayor of new orleans we  should know mark is married to cbs this  morning saturday co-host michelle miller  that's a good thing because we love  michelle so mark good morning thanks for  joining us  uh mark you heard the number that tony  just said 55 percent of americans in our  poll  say they approve of presidents biden's  handling of race relations  how do you think he's doing  first of all thank you for having me  good morning anthony the  president has been purposeful and  intentional  about including racial justice  components in  every policy he's undertaken in the  first 100 years  in covit he's expanded community-based  vaccination sites in the cabinet  with vice president harris and all the  way through he's had the most  diverse cabinet in modern american  history in the american rescue plan  there are anti-poverty provisions and  provisions to assist  businesses owned by people of color  including african americans  and his commitment to racial justice his  forward-leaning  support for the george floyd justice and  policing act the minimum wage  along with the idea that infrastructure  and this is a recommendation we made to  him both as a candidate  and in the transition has to include  investments in human beings  so this president's start is purposeful  it's intentional and  the support amongst the public i think  demonstrates  that the president has used his moral  voice to create a distinction between  the poisonous philosophy of white  supremacy and the idea of  an america for everyone in america with  opportunity  and pathways to progress for all people  mark  the president said he would prioritize  racial justice and you said  you'd hold him to that what more would  you like to see  well first of all we need uh kristen  clarke to be confirmed as the chief  civil  rights enforcer as assistant attorney  general for civil rights  number two we need passage of the  justice and policing act  the george floyd justice and policing  act we need a  minimum wage increase which is long  overdue we haven't had a national  increase  since 2007 and we need an  assault on this pandemic of voter  suppression now with 250 bills  being introduced all across the nation  the weight of the presidency  we need the weight of the justice  department uh and we also need  cooperation uh from uh from what i would  call some  the members of the united states senate  who seem to be held bent on blocking  progress the president's numbers  demonstrate  uh and his electoral victory  demonstrates that he has i think a broad  base of support amongst the american  people it's not universal  but it's broad and i think it's deep  because people want accomplishments  they want progress and i think on race  relations uh president biden  i believe has gotten off to a powerful  and a purposeful start but there is a  lot left to be done anthony this  hundred day if you will mark is sort of  an artificial deadline  but since uh it's uh it's part of our  sort of tradition going back  to president franklin roosevelt it's  important it's an important time to talk  about  uh where the president is and where the  nation is and i'll say this  the other important thing is the  president has changed the tone  of governing to an inclusive tone of  governing away from  a reality show finger-pointing uh  disparagement tone that existed for the  last four years  and i think that creates pathways for  him to achieve real progress we will  continue to hold him accountable  but so far the start is purposeful well  as you know mark the justice department  is now investigating not one but two  police departments some say that's a  sign that they're taking it seriously  president biden is urging congress to  pass legislation  oh there he is there you are mark we  lost you for just a second  so you know it's a very tense time  between the  communities of color and the police  department police departments all over  the country  what do you think needs to be done how  can we effectively address that issue  and what should team biden be doing  about that at this particular time  so i think that mayor garland and the  department of justice have taken two  important steps  just in the last week and that that is  to engage in pattern and  practice uh investigations both in  minneapolis  as well as as in louisville that means  that the president  and his justice department are going to  use the full weight and the full  toolbox available to them on the issue  of  policing and improving trust but yep  these killings are gut-wrenching they're  hurtful i have a 19 year old son  when i see these young men i shudder i i  feel a sense of great pain like all of  us do  but this is what's important the  president the justice department  the national government cannot fix the  problem of policing alone  it takes every community every mayor  city council  county exec states like maryland have  put a marker down with the statewide law  everyone has to be in this because this  system is broken  this system uh uh for the most part uh  has lost  great faith amongst the american people  and we need to restore it  but we need a new vision on how we keep  communities safe  yeah well you can't fix it if you don't  acknowledge it it at least appears that  the biden administration is  acknowledging houston we have a big  problem mark marielle it's always good  to see you  our best in the show yes sir you're  gonna see her before we do he's the best  yeah we think she's the best thanks a  lot we will bring you a special report  on the president's address to congress  that starts tonight at nine o'clock  eastern  right here on cbs

